Release the GIL while the context manager runs its SQL.

Connection.__exit__ commits through connection_context_manager_exec,
which called sqlite3_exec with the GIL held. The commit of the outer
savepoint writes the WAL and may checkpoint and fsync, so a slow disk
froze every Python thread: 39s on btrfs, measured. Lillecarl/pymux#936.

Every callback that sqlite3_exec can reach takes the GIL itself, as it
does for sqlite3_step in cursor.c.

--- a/src/connection.c
+++ b/src/connection.c
@@ -4868,7 +4868,9 @@
   }

   char *errmsg = NULL;
-  int res = sqlite3_exec(self->db, sql, 0, 0, &errmsg);
+  int res;
+  Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS res = sqlite3_exec(self->db, sql, 0, 0, &errmsg);
+  Py_END_ALLOW_THREADS;
   if (res != SQLITE_OK)
   {
     /* in these cases we won't create an exception */
